WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.012
The Meta Pixel feature connects your site to Meta, so you can track

00:00:05.012 --> 00:00:07.046
conversions from your Meta ads.

00:00:07.046 --> 00:00:11.442
In this video, you enable it, add your Pixel Identifier, and configure the

00:00:11.442 --> 00:00:14.630
Conversions API, which sends events from the server.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.732
When you run ads on Meta, you want to know what visitors do after they click.

00:00:23.732 --> 00:00:28.180
The Meta Pixel feature reports their visits and actions to Meta in two ways.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.913
The pixel runs in the browser of each visitor.

00:00:31.913 --> 00:00:36.273
The Conversions API runs on your server, so it also covers events that

00:00:36.273 --> 00:00:38.281
do not depend on the browser.

00:00:38.281 --> 00:00:41.150
Both use the Pixel Identifier of your pixel.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.062
Open Tools, Features.

00:00:47.062 --> 00:00:49.093
Let's search for Meta Pixel.

00:00:49.093 --> 00:00:53.990
The result is listed under the Meta category, which holds the Meta features.

00:00:54.472 --> 00:00:58.301
The description of Meta Pixel says it provides a way to enable Meta

00:00:58.301 --> 00:01:00.137
Pixel tracking for your site.

00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:01.502
Let's select Enable.

00:01:01.965 --> 00:01:06.429
The site restarts its features, and the Meta Pixel row now shows the

00:01:06.429 --> 00:01:08.902
Enabled badge and a Disable button.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:13.125
The feature adds a Meta Pixel page under Settings, Integrations.

00:01:17.425 --> 00:01:20.249
Open Settings, Integrations, Meta Pixel.

00:01:20.249 --> 00:01:25.957
The Pixel Identifier field comes first, and its hint says where to find the identifier:

00:01:25.957 --> 00:01:28.105
the Meta Data Sources portal.

00:01:28.571 --> 00:01:30.885
Let's enter the identifier of the pixel.

00:01:30.885 --> 00:01:35.411
This example uses a made-up value, and you enter the one from your own Meta account.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:36.990
Select Save.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.457
A notice confirms:

00:01:38.457 --> 00:01:40.962
Site settings updated successfully.

00:01:40.962 --> 00:01:45.850
Once the settings are saved, the pixel is added to the pages of your site.

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:53.924
The pixel is added to the pages that visitors see, and it reports

00:01:53.924 --> 00:01:56.434
a page view each time one of them loads.

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:58.670
It is not added to the admin pages.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.855
If the Pixel Identifier is empty, no pixel is added to your pages.

00:02:03.855 --> 00:02:08.597
And when tracking is not allowed for a visitor, Orchard Core leaves the

00:02:08.597 --> 00:02:11.060
pixel out of that visitor's pages.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.853
Below the Pixel Identifier, the Conversions API section is optional.

00:02:19.853 --> 00:02:24.588
It delivers events to Meta from the server, so workflows can send events

00:02:24.588 --> 00:02:26.470
such as Purchase or Lead.

00:02:26.923 --> 00:02:30.108
The Conversions API Access Token is a password field.

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:34.426
You generate the token for your dataset in the Events Manager, and Orchard

00:02:34.426 --> 00:02:36.693
Core encrypts it before it saves it.

00:02:37.146 --> 00:02:41.096
The Test Event Code is optional, and comes from the Test Events tab

00:02:41.096 --> 00:02:42.611
of the Events Manager.

00:02:42.611 --> 00:02:47.806
While it is set, events appear in the test console instead of counting as production data.

00:02:48.261 --> 00:02:51.221
Remove the Test Event Code before you go live.

00:02:51.221 --> 00:02:53.441
Select Save to keep the settings.

00:02:57.641 --> 00:03:00.846
The settings do not send events by themselves.

00:03:00.846 --> 00:03:02.202
A workflow does.

00:03:02.202 --> 00:03:07.995
Enable the Workflows feature, open Design, Workflows, and the workflow editor offers the

00:03:07.995 --> 00:03:11.631
Meta Conversions API Event task in its Meta category.

00:03:12.381 --> 00:03:14.270
Event Name is required:

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.504
a standard name such as Purchase or Lead, or a custom name.

00:03:18.504 --> 00:03:24.431
Action Source tells Meta where the conversion took place, and Website is the default.

00:03:25.181 --> 00:03:27.202
Event Source URL is optional:

00:03:27.202 --> 00:03:30.147
the browser address where the event happened.

00:03:30.147 --> 00:03:31.936
Event ID is optional too.

00:03:31.936 --> 00:03:35.920
It is a unique id that lets Meta deduplicate the event when the

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.691
browser pixel also reports the same event.

00:03:39.441 --> 00:03:42.114
The task ends with one of two outcomes.

00:03:42.114 --> 00:03:46.509
Done means Meta accepted the event, and Failed means it did not, for

00:03:46.509 --> 00:03:50.726
example when the Pixel Identifier or the access token is missing.

00:03:50.726 --> 00:03:53.101
A separate video covers workflows.

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:58.852
The Meta Pixel feature adds your pixel to the pages of your site,

00:03:58.852 --> 00:04:02.708
and the Conversions API lets workflows send events from the server.

00:04:02.708 --> 00:04:04.081
Thanks for watching.

